WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.049
Deep-compression tokenizers make diffusion cheap,

2
00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:04.464
but their latents are hard to learn.

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.374
Semantic encoders like DINOv2 train fast,

4
00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.940
but at 32× they lose pixel detail.

5
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.323
Meet DC-SAE.

6
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.880
A frozen semantic encoder reads a downsampled image.

7
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.166
A pixel encoder keeps texture and color.

8
00:00:20.265 --> 00:00:22.897
Both fuse into one compact latent for diffusion.

9
00:00:22.997 --> 00:00:25.719
A decoder-side DeMerger restores dense detail.

10
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.309
Faces and text stay intact at 32×.

11
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:38.021
On ImageNet 512, gFID drops to 3.37,

12
00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:40.450
and PSNR rises 3.5 dB.

13
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.503
And the autoencoder is 4.75× faster.

14
00:00:47.499 --> 00:00:49.415
The joint latent also converges faster.

15
00:00:52.099 --> 00:00:55.205
It scales to text-to-image: 0.84 on GenEval.

16
00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:01.565
DC-SAE. High compression, fast convergence.

